14. Impact of Data Quality on Logistics Analytics
High-quality data is essential for reliable logistics decision-making. Missing values can reduce the usefulness of important variables and cause problems during modeling. Duplicate records can lead to incorrect order counts and inflated business metrics. Invalid values can produce unrealistic results, while unexamined outliers can strongly influence statistical models.
The preprocessing performed in this project improves the reliability of future analysis by ensuring that the dataset has no remaining missing values or duplicate records and that important numerical and date fields have been validated. The engineered schedule_deviation feature provides a direct measure of delivery schedule performance and can support future delivery-delay analysis.
15. Reflection
This preprocessing exercise demonstrated that data preparation is not simply a technical step but an important part of logistics decision-making. Raw logistics data may contain significant amounts of incomplete or inconsistent information, and each issue needs to be handled according to its business meaning.
For example, removing Product Description was reasonable because the entire column was missing. Similarly, replacing a small number of missing customer values with 'Unknown' preserved useful records without significantly affecting the dataset. The outlier analysis also showed why automated deletion can be dangerous. A high order value or unusual profit may be a legitimate business transaction rather than an error. Therefore, outliers should be investigated in their business context before being removed.
16. Conclusion
The Week 2 task successfully implemented a complete data collection, cleaning, and preprocessing workflow for logistics analysis using Python. The final dataset contains 180,519 rows and 58 columns, with zero missing values and zero duplicate records after preprocessing and feature engineering. Missing-value treatment, invalid-value validation, IQR-based outlier detection, date conversion, feature engineering, schedule deviation calculation, and Min-Max normalization were successfully demonstrated.
The resulting processed dataset provides a reliable foundation for subsequent exploratory data analysis, visualization, predictive modeling, and logistics optimization. The next stage of the internship can use this cleaned dataset to identify operational patterns, investigate delivery delays, compare regions and shipping modes, and develop analytical insights for logistics decision-making.
